<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calpulli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as calpōlli, calpul, parcialidades, barrios</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Aztec society a calpulli (Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> calpōlli, 'large houses') was a unit of commoner housing within a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> or altepetl, organised as a kin-based or other land-holding group; Spanish sources call them parcialidades or barrios. A calpul could rest on an extended family, a shared ethnic background or similar skills and tribute demands. Its members coordinated land use, food production and the making of tribute, paid in goods or labour, and shared organisational and religious tasks. The calpul also educated the young, running the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telpochcalli">Tēlpochcalli</a> schools where young men learned to be warriors, and its men went to war for their calpul.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nobles received tribute and the labour of commoners, held estates, wore fine garments forbidden to others and were schooled for war, office and the priesthood, while commoners worked the land of their <b>calpolli</b> and paid tribute in goods and labour.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
